WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:06.209
Welcome to part three, the hydraulic
system of the A320, chapter 29.

2
00:00:06.259 --> 00:00:09.868
The exam scope stops after the
Green system, so we cover the

3
00:00:09.908 --> 00:00:13.396
architecture shared by the
three systems, the controls and

4
00:00:13.436 --> 00:00:16.680
indications, and then follow
the Green system from its

5
00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.145
reservoir to its users; the detailed
Yellow and Blue pages are out.

6
00:00:22.795 --> 00:00:27.986
The A320 has three independent
hydraulic systems, named by colour.

7
00:00:28.036 --> 00:00:31.121
Green works as the main
system: it has a pump driven

8
00:00:31.161 --> 00:00:34.876
by engine 1, the most users
and the biggest reservoir.

9
00:00:34.926 --> 00:00:38.209
Yellow behaves as an alternate
system: an engine-driven

10
00:00:38.249 --> 00:00:42.103
pump on engine 2, plus an
electric pump and a hand pump.

11
00:00:42.153 --> 00:00:45.368
Blue is the emergency system,
with an electric pump and

12
00:00:45.408 --> 00:00:48.564
the ram air turbine; Airbus
does not use these labels,

13
00:00:48.604 --> 00:00:52.209
but compared with other
aircraft those are their roles.

14
00:00:52.259 --> 00:00:54.932
All three work at 3000 psi, and the

15
00:00:54.972 --> 00:00:58.669
engine-driven pumps are of the
variable displacement type.

16
00:00:58.719 --> 00:01:03.168
Each system has its own reservoir,
pumps, pipes and users, so a leak in one

17
00:01:03.208 --> 00:01:07.716
cannot drain another; the only link is
the power transfer unit between Green

18
00:01:07.756 --> 00:01:13.373
and Yellow, which is mechanical and acts
at a pressure difference of 500 psi.

19
00:01:13.423 --> 00:01:16.244
And a detail the quiz
tests: all three reservoirs

20
00:01:16.284 --> 00:01:19.787
are filled from the service
panel of the Green system.

21
00:01:21.437 --> 00:01:26.884
This deck page lists the users of each
system, read upwards from each reservoir.

22
00:01:26.934 --> 00:01:30.889
Green, the biggest, supplies
the landing gear and its doors,

23
00:01:30.929 --> 00:01:34.817
the nose-wheel steering, the
normal brakes, the reverser of

24
00:01:34.857 --> 00:01:38.945
engine 1, the flap and slat
motors, the trimmable horizontal

25
00:01:38.985 --> 00:01:43.863
stabiliser, the rudder, a yaw damper
and many flight control servos.

26
00:01:43.913 --> 00:01:47.968
Yellow supplies the reverser of
engine 2, the flaps, the cargo

27
00:01:48.008 --> 00:01:52.262
doors, the alternate brakes and
its share of flight controls.

28
00:01:52.312 --> 00:01:56.497
Blue supplies the constant speed motor
generator, which is the emergency

29
00:01:56.537 --> 00:02:01.230
electrical generator, the slats and
the rest of its flight control servos.

30
00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.828
The flight controls show the
redundancy philosophy: each

31
00:02:04.868 --> 00:02:08.805
aileron has two servo actuators,
one Blue and one Green.

32
00:02:08.855 --> 00:02:12.675
And the elevators: on the left
one servo is Green and the other

33
00:02:12.715 --> 00:02:16.351
Blue, on the right one Blue and
one Yellow, so pitch control

34
00:02:16.391 --> 00:02:20.908
survives the loss of any single
system; fail safe, as the notes say.

35
00:02:22.558 --> 00:02:24.928
Let us look at the power sources.

36
00:02:24.978 --> 00:02:28.298
The engine-driven pumps, Green
on engine 1 and Yellow on

37
00:02:28.338 --> 00:02:31.425
engine 2, sit on the accessory
gearbox, so they turn

38
00:02:31.465 --> 00:02:35.870
whenever the engine turns, even when
they are not delivering pressure.

39
00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:40.561
They are variable displacement pumps:
pistons in a rotating barrel, like the

40
00:02:40.601 --> 00:02:44.935
cylinder of a revolver, rest on a
plate whose tilt is set by a solenoid

41
00:02:44.975 --> 00:02:50.171
valve; tilted, the pistons stroke and
deliver fluid at 3000 psi, and with

42
00:02:50.211 --> 00:02:55.612
the plate neutral there is no flow, which
is the state with the pushbutton at OFF.

43
00:02:55.662 --> 00:03:00.301
They are fed from the reservoir at 50
psi so that they never cavitate, and the

44
00:03:00.341 --> 00:03:05.370
same fluid lubricates and cools the pump
and returns through the case-drain filter.

45
00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:08.461
The alternating current
electric pumps are a backup on

46
00:03:08.501 --> 00:03:11.657
Yellow, also used for the
cargo doors, and the main pump

47
00:03:11.697 --> 00:03:16.497
of the Blue system; they take
fluid at 50 psi and deliver 3000.

48
00:03:16.547 --> 00:03:19.996
The minimum equipment list
treats them differently: without

49
00:03:20.036 --> 00:03:23.366
the Blue electric pump the
aircraft cannot fly, while the

50
00:03:23.406 --> 00:03:27.091
Yellow one is a category C item,
10 days to repair; since they

51
00:03:27.131 --> 00:03:31.561
are identical, a failed Blue pump
can be swapped with the Yellow one.

52
00:03:31.611 --> 00:03:34.288
Finally Yellow has a hand
pump, to operate the

53
00:03:34.328 --> 00:03:37.394
cargo doors when there
is no electrical power.

54
00:03:39.044 --> 00:03:41.728
Three more elements
complete the power side.

55
00:03:41.778 --> 00:03:45.032
The ram air turbine is the
emergency source of the Blue

56
00:03:45.072 --> 00:03:48.266
system: a two-blade turbine
stowed in the left ventral

57
00:03:48.306 --> 00:03:51.740
fuselage whose variable-pitch
blades hold a constant speed

58
00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:55.214
whatever the airspeed, the
timing and rpm figures being on

59
00:03:55.254 --> 00:03:58.509
a later page beyond the exam
scope, driving a Blue pump

60
00:03:58.549 --> 00:04:02.852
and, through the constant speed
motor generator, electrical power.

61
00:04:02.902 --> 00:04:06.892
It is deployed by a compressed
spring pack, released when either

62
00:04:06.932 --> 00:04:10.858
of two solenoid valves is energised,
automatically on a loss of

63
00:04:10.898 --> 00:04:15.593
electrical power, or manually with
the guarded RAT MAN ON pushbutton.

64
00:04:15.643 --> 00:04:22.314
Accumulators hold fluid against a
nitrogen pre-charge, 1885 psi at 20

65
00:04:22.354 --> 00:04:26.055
degrees in the one-litre Green
accumulator, so that when a user

66
00:04:26.095 --> 00:04:30.958
suddenly demands flow, the pressure drop
is absorbed before the pump reacts.

67
00:04:31.008 --> 00:04:34.400
The power transfer unit is a
hydraulic motor and a pump on a

68
00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:38.004
common shaft, one on the Green
side and one on the Yellow side:

69
00:04:38.044 --> 00:04:42.008
with its pushbutton in AUTO,
when one system is more than 500

70
00:04:42.048 --> 00:04:45.726
psi below the other, the healthy
system drives the motor, which

71
00:04:45.766 --> 00:04:50.120
turns the pump of the weak system,
transferring power but never fluid.

72
00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:53.412
It is inhibited during engine
start and when the cargo doors

73
00:04:53.452 --> 00:04:56.367
are operated with the Yellow
electric pump, so that an

74
00:04:56.407 --> 00:05:00.586
aircraft on the ground does not start
transferring power unexpectedly.

75
00:05:02.236 --> 00:05:04.778
The reservoirs are
pressurised with air, and

76
00:05:04.818 --> 00:05:07.985
that is where the two
systems of this exam meet.

77
00:05:08.035 --> 00:05:12.211
Each reservoir is pressurised
to 50 psi, 3.5 bar, so

78
00:05:12.251 --> 00:05:15.734
that the pumps are always
primed and never cavitate.

79
00:05:15.784 --> 00:05:20.638
The air normally comes from stage 8 of
the high pressure compressor of engine 1.

80
00:05:20.688 --> 00:05:23.738
If engine 1 is lost, the
pneumatic system supplies

81
00:05:23.778 --> 00:05:27.099
it, and on the ground a
ground supply can be used.

82
00:05:27.149 --> 00:05:30.296
The reservoirs are sealed,
so they keep their pressure

83
00:05:30.336 --> 00:05:33.873
after a pressurisation failure
or an engine shut-down.

84
00:05:33.923 --> 00:05:38.347
If the air pressure falls below
22 psi in Green or Yellow,

85
00:05:38.387 --> 00:05:43.201
or 30 psi in Blue, the ECAM shows
a low air pressure warning.

86
00:05:44.851 --> 00:05:47.840
The crew controls the
hydraulics from overhead panel

87
00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:51.784
40VU, next to the air conditioning
and pneumatic panels.

88
00:05:51.834 --> 00:05:55.492
The engine pump pushbuttons,
ENG 1 PUMP for Green and ENG

89
00:05:55.532 --> 00:05:58.906
2 PUMP for Yellow, are dark
when the pump is pressurising;

90
00:05:58.946 --> 00:06:02.149
a white OFF means the pump is
turning with the engine but

91
00:06:02.189 --> 00:06:05.953
not delivering pressure, and an
amber FAULT means a failure.

92
00:06:06.003 --> 00:06:10.097
The Yellow electric pump shows a blue
ON when it has been switched on, a

93
00:06:10.137 --> 00:06:14.735
non-normal state that must be cancelled
later, and amber FAULT on failure.

94
00:06:14.785 --> 00:06:18.101
The Blue electric pump pushbutton
sits in AUTO, and, with

95
00:06:18.141 --> 00:06:21.575
electrical power available, the
pump runs when an engine is

96
00:06:21.615 --> 00:06:25.342
running, or in flight, which the
system knows from the nose-gear

97
00:06:25.382 --> 00:06:30.089
shock absorber being extended, or when
the BLUE PUMP OVERRIDE is selected.

98
00:06:30.139 --> 00:06:35.621
The PTU pushbutton arms the power transfer
unit in AUTO, with its own amber FAULT.

99
00:06:35.671 --> 00:06:39.538
The RAT MAN ON pushbutton is
protected by a red guard.

100
00:06:39.588 --> 00:06:43.281
A second panel further aft holds
four guarded pushbuttons: BLUE

101
00:06:43.321 --> 00:06:46.954
PUMP OVERRIDE, used by maintenance
to run the Blue pump on the

102
00:06:46.994 --> 00:06:51.490
ground, and the three leak measurement
valves, Green, Blue and Yellow.

103
00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:58.285
Pressing HYD on the ECAM control panel
brings up this page on the lower display.

104
00:06:58.335 --> 00:07:01.374
Each system has its label
and its pressure from a

105
00:07:01.414 --> 00:07:06.789
transmitter, green when normal,
around 3000 psi, and amber when low.

106
00:07:06.839 --> 00:07:10.338
Each pump has a symbol: a square
in line with the supply line

107
00:07:10.378 --> 00:07:13.876
and green when delivering pressure;
across the line and amber

108
00:07:13.916 --> 00:07:17.241
when the pump is selected off;
and an amber LO when it is

109
00:07:17.281 --> 00:07:23.664
selected on but the pressure switch
after it reads less than 1740 psi.

110
00:07:23.714 --> 00:07:27.618
The reservoir shows its level
with an arrow, green at the box

111
00:07:27.658 --> 00:07:31.174
when normal, lower and amber
when low, plus an overheat

112
00:07:31.214 --> 00:07:35.183
indication when the returning
fluid is too hot, which suggests

113
00:07:35.223 --> 00:07:40.875
heavy internal leakage, and LO AIR PRESS
when the reservoir pressurisation falls.

114
00:07:40.925 --> 00:07:45.129
The fire valves of Green and Yellow
are shown in line and green when

115
00:07:45.169 --> 00:07:49.498
open and across and amber when fully
closed, and the PTU and RAT are

116
00:07:49.538 --> 00:07:54.568
white when available, amber while
changing state, and green when supplying.

117
00:07:56.218 --> 00:08:01.677
And an exercise with the HYD page:
picture it before each answer appears.

118
00:08:01.727 --> 00:08:03.841
One: normal flight.

119
00:08:03.891 --> 00:08:08.122
The three system labels are green
at about 3000 psi, the Green

120
00:08:08.162 --> 00:08:11.643
and Yellow engine pumps are drawn
in line and green, the fire

121
00:08:11.683 --> 00:08:16.188
valves are open, and the PTU and the
RAT are white, meaning available.

122
00:08:16.238 --> 00:08:20.977
Two: the Green engine-driven pump
has failed, with the PTU in AUTO.

123
00:08:21.027 --> 00:08:25.201
The Green pump shows an amber LO,
the PTU symbol turns green with its

124
00:08:25.241 --> 00:08:31.268
arrow pointing towards Green, and Green
holds about 2 500 psi, still green.

125
00:08:31.318 --> 00:08:34.884
Three: the engine 1 fire
pushbutton is pushed.

126
00:08:34.934 --> 00:08:37.739
The Green fire valve is
drawn across the line, in

127
00:08:37.779 --> 00:08:41.031
amber, fully closed, and
the Green pump shows LO.

128
00:08:41.081 --> 00:08:43.788
Four: the Green reservoir is leaking.

129
00:08:43.838 --> 00:08:47.500
The reservoir arrow drops below the
box and turns amber when the low

130
00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:51.584
level is reached at 3 litres, while
an overheat is a different failure:

131
00:08:51.624 --> 00:08:56.112
heavy internal leakage heats the returning
fluid without lowering the level.

132
00:08:57.762 --> 00:09:00.251
In the aircraft, every
pipe carries a coded

133
00:09:00.291 --> 00:09:04.230
band, and this slide shows
the A320 hydraulic code.

134
00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:06.791
There are supply and return lines, and the

135
00:09:06.831 --> 00:09:09.854
return lines are usually
of larger diameter.

136
00:09:09.904 --> 00:09:13.605
The colour combination identifies
a hydraulic line, as opposed

137
00:09:13.645 --> 00:09:17.917
to fuel; pneumatic ducts are much
thicker and easy to tell apart.

138
00:09:17.967 --> 00:09:22.787
Arrows show the direction of flow,
and black dots mark a pressure line.

139
00:09:22.837 --> 00:09:29.236
A numbered band identifies the system:
1 is Green, 2 is Blue and 3 is Yellow.

140
00:09:29.286 --> 00:09:34.059
And a part number lets the mechanic
find exactly that pipe in the manuals.

141
00:09:35.709 --> 00:09:40.483
The leak measurement valves deserve their
own slide because the quiz uses them.

142
00:09:40.533 --> 00:09:44.216
In each system, a leak measurement
valve isolates the group of

143
00:09:44.256 --> 00:09:48.870
primary flight control users from the
rest of the users of that system.

144
00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:53.486
Normally it is open, so the flight
controls are permanently supplied through

145
00:09:53.526 --> 00:09:57.971
it; it closes for the leak test, and
on Yellow also automatically when the

146
00:09:58.011 --> 00:10:03.028
cargo doors are operated with the electric
pump, so that no surface can move.

147
00:10:03.078 --> 00:10:07.452
Leaks are tested because the return flow
equals the demand plus the permanent

148
00:10:07.492 --> 00:10:12.658
internal leakage of every component, which
flows even when the users are in neutral.

149
00:10:12.708 --> 00:10:16.581
The test uses an external hydraulic
power unit with a flowmeter;

150
00:10:16.621 --> 00:10:20.372
the internal leakage is read on
the flowmeter, and closing the

151
00:10:20.412 --> 00:10:23.919
valves section by section,
right wing, left wing, tail and

152
00:10:23.959 --> 00:10:28.650
centre, locates the guilty group by
comparison with the allowed values.

153
00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:33.130
Excess leakage heats the fluid and
degrades the system; when a servo control

154
00:10:33.170 --> 00:10:37.990
is suspected, it is disconnected and
the section is re-tested to confirm it.

155
00:10:39.640 --> 00:10:41.869
Now we follow the fluid through the Green

156
00:10:41.909 --> 00:10:44.970
system, which is the last
part of the exam scope.

157
00:10:45.020 --> 00:10:49.001
The reservoir holds 18 litres
at most, about 14 in normal

158
00:10:49.041 --> 00:10:52.381
operation, with a low level
warning at 3 litres; it is

159
00:10:52.421 --> 00:10:57.375
pressurised to 50 psi with a low
air pressure warning at 22 psi.

160
00:10:57.425 --> 00:11:01.547
The filling equipment of this system
serves all three: a selector valve

161
00:11:01.587 --> 00:11:06.158
chooses whether the hand pump fills
the Green, Yellow or Blue reservoir.

162
00:11:06.208 --> 00:11:10.466
From the reservoir, the fluid passes
the fire valve, located in the left

163
00:11:10.506 --> 00:11:15.181
pylon and driven by an electric motor;
when the crew pushes the ENG 1 FIRE

164
00:11:15.221 --> 00:11:20.048
pushbutton, it closes and isolates the
Green fluid from the burning engine.

165
00:11:20.098 --> 00:11:24.376
Then comes the engine-driven pump,
3000 psi, self-regulating and

166
00:11:24.416 --> 00:11:27.897
of variable displacement, whose
case-drain flow returns to the

167
00:11:27.937 --> 00:11:31.590
reservoir through its own filter,
with a clogging indication at a

168
00:11:31.630 --> 00:11:36.418
pressure difference of 87 psi,
not to be confused with the 3000

169
00:11:36.458 --> 00:11:40.167
psi system pressure; a pressure
switch after it triggers the LO

170
00:11:40.207 --> 00:11:46.919
indication below 1740 psi, and the line
also feeds the reverser of engine 1.

171
00:11:48.569 --> 00:11:50.810
The pump delivers into the high pressure

172
00:11:50.850 --> 00:11:53.547
manifold, a block that
carries most of the Green

173
00:11:53.587 --> 00:11:57.244
system's small components, all
with numbers worth knowing.

174
00:11:57.294 --> 00:12:00.738
The high pressure filter has a
clogging indicator that pops out

175
00:12:00.778 --> 00:12:05.387
at a pressure difference of 87
psi, and is replaced when it does.

176
00:12:05.437 --> 00:12:09.873
There is a check valve, and a sample
port valve to take fluid samples, which

177
00:12:09.913 --> 00:12:14.739
the laboratory analyses for water and
particles that reveal worn components.

178
00:12:14.789 --> 00:12:17.571
A pressure transducer
sends the pressure to the

179
00:12:17.611 --> 00:12:21.385
ELAC flight control computers
and to the ECAM display.

180
00:12:21.435 --> 00:12:26.110
A pressure switch informs the ELAC,
SEC and FAC flight computers, the

181
00:12:26.150 --> 00:12:33.929
brake and steering control unit, the
BSCU, and the ECAM; it trips at 1450 psi.

182
00:12:33.979 --> 00:12:37.780
The relief valve opens at 3500 psi to

183
00:12:37.820 --> 00:12:42.630
protect the system and
closes again at 3200 psi.

184
00:12:42.680 --> 00:12:45.448
Finally the leak measurement
valve, and outside the

185
00:12:45.488 --> 00:12:48.312
manifold the accumulator:
a one-litre steel cylinder

186
00:12:48.352 --> 00:12:55.035
wrapped in Kevlar with a nitrogen
pre-charge of 1885 psi at 20 degrees.

187
00:12:56.685 --> 00:12:59.425
The last block of the
Green system is the PTU

188
00:12:59.465 --> 00:13:02.596
manifold, with its own
dedicated set of valves.

189
00:13:02.646 --> 00:13:05.914
The normal brake selector valve
supplies the normal braking

190
00:13:05.954 --> 00:13:10.229
system and cuts it off, putting it
to return, when there is a problem.

191
00:13:10.279 --> 00:13:14.200
A solenoid valve, open with the
PTU pushbutton in AUTO, cuts the

192
00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:18.431
operation of the power transfer
unit when the pushbutton is off.

193
00:13:18.481 --> 00:13:21.431
Two check valves complete the PTU path.

194
00:13:21.481 --> 00:13:24.662
The priority valve, marked
P, isolates the large-flow

195
00:13:24.702 --> 00:13:27.639
users, the landing gear
and doors, the nose-wheel

196
00:13:27.679 --> 00:13:30.921
steering and the slat and
flap motors, when the system

197
00:13:30.961 --> 00:13:34.263
pressure drops, so that all
the pressure available goes

198
00:13:34.303 --> 00:13:38.724
to the primary flight controls and
the aircraft stays controllable.

199
00:13:38.774 --> 00:13:42.571
The fluid returns through the
return manifold, with a temperature

200
00:13:42.611 --> 00:13:47.448
transmitter and a return filter
of 29 psi, back to the reservoir.

201
00:13:49.098 --> 00:13:51.993
Back on the Green schematic,
the top block is the leak

202
00:13:52.033 --> 00:13:55.915
measurement manifold, and it is
worth knowing who sits behind it.

203
00:13:55.965 --> 00:14:00.074
Through the leak measurement valve,
it feeds the Green primary flight

204
00:14:00.114 --> 00:14:04.732
control users, split into three groups:
tail, left wing and right wing.

205
00:14:04.782 --> 00:14:07.966
The tail group holds the
hydraulic motor that drives the

206
00:14:08.006 --> 00:14:11.074
trimmable horizontal stabiliser
screw, the rudder, and

207
00:14:11.114 --> 00:14:14.687
one of the yaw damper actuators
that prevent Dutch roll.

208
00:14:14.737 --> 00:14:17.296
The left wing group holds
one of the two servo

209
00:14:17.336 --> 00:14:20.567
actuators of the aileron
and spoilers 1 and 5.

210
00:14:20.617 --> 00:14:23.568
The right wing group is the
mirror image on the right.

211
00:14:23.618 --> 00:14:27.064
The spool valves drawn at the top
are normally closed and are used

212
00:14:27.104 --> 00:14:31.363
only during maintenance, to check the
internal leakage section by section.

213
00:14:33.013 --> 00:14:35.233
Finally, where all of this lives.

214
00:14:35.283 --> 00:14:38.410
Almost every Green component
is in the main landing

215
00:14:38.450 --> 00:14:41.640
gear bay: opening the gear
door is enough to see the

216
00:14:41.680 --> 00:14:45.942
reservoir, the high pressure
and PTU manifolds and the PTU.

217
00:14:45.992 --> 00:14:49.923
The reservoir is made of two welded
metal cylinders, with a drain valve

218
00:14:49.963 --> 00:14:53.725
at the bottom, an air manifold
carrying a pressure gauge, a pressure

219
00:14:53.765 --> 00:14:58.533
switch and a check valve, and a direct-
reading quantity indicator on its side.

220
00:14:58.583 --> 00:15:02.100
Its low level sensor is a
cylindrical capacitor in which the

221
00:15:02.140 --> 00:15:05.834
hydraulic fluid acts as the
dielectric: as the quantity changes

222
00:15:05.874 --> 00:15:10.670
so does the capacitance, and at 3
litres it sends the low level signal.

223
00:15:12.320 --> 00:15:15.063
This last Green page
shows the PTU manifold

224
00:15:15.103 --> 00:15:18.359
on the left and the service
panel on the right.

225
00:15:18.409 --> 00:15:22.371
On the PTU manifold you can recognise
the priority valve, the two

226
00:15:22.411 --> 00:15:26.643
check valves and the solenoid valve
that we met in the schematic.

227
00:15:26.693 --> 00:15:30.131
The service panel, behind the
left main gear door, has the

228
00:15:30.171 --> 00:15:33.610
reservoir filling selector
valve, to choose Green, Blue or

229
00:15:33.650 --> 00:15:38.258
Yellow, and a quantity indicator with
a coloured range for each system.

230
00:15:38.308 --> 00:15:42.066
Its filling port with a filter and
its filling hand pump, not to be

231
00:15:42.106 --> 00:15:46.092
confused with the Yellow hand pump
of the cargo doors, are used to fill

232
00:15:46.132 --> 00:15:51.301
all three reservoirs, which is the fact
behind statement b of the 2020 quiz.

233
00:15:51.351 --> 00:15:55.424
It also has the suction and pressure
ports to connect a hydraulic ground

234
00:15:55.464 --> 00:16:00.327
cart, the mule, which bypasses the Green
pump for maintenance and hangar tests.

235
00:16:01.977 --> 00:16:05.250
To finish the Green system,
let us trace failures the

236
00:16:05.290 --> 00:16:09.015
way an exam statement does;
pause after each question.

237
00:16:09.065 --> 00:16:12.036
One: the Green engine-driven
pump fails in flight

238
00:16:12.076 --> 00:16:15.684
with the PTU in AUTO; what
is the first indication?

239
00:16:15.734 --> 00:16:18.695
The pressure switch at the
pump outlet reads less than

240
00:16:18.735 --> 00:16:24.420
1740 psi, and the ECAM shows
the amber LO on the Green pump.

241
00:16:24.470 --> 00:16:25.505
And then?

242
00:16:25.555 --> 00:16:30.164
As soon as Green is 500 psi below
Yellow, the Yellow system drives the

243
00:16:30.204 --> 00:16:37.282
PTU, which holds Green at about 2
500 psi, so the 1450 psi manifold

244
00:16:37.322 --> 00:16:42.263
switch is never reached, and the brakes
and the priority valve are not affected.

245
00:16:42.313 --> 00:16:46.247
Three: the same failure, but with
the PTU off or inhibited, or

246
00:16:46.287 --> 00:16:52.660
with the Green fluid lost, so the
pressure falls below 1450 psi.

247
00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:56.847
The manifold pressure switch tells the
brake and steering control unit, which

248
00:16:56.887 --> 00:17:01.686
loses the normal brakes and switches to
the alternate brakes on the Yellow system.

249
00:17:01.736 --> 00:17:04.594
Four: and as the pressure keeps falling?

250
00:17:04.644 --> 00:17:07.967
The priority valve shuts off
the heavy users, the landing

251
00:17:08.007 --> 00:17:11.566
gear and doors, the nose-wheel
steering and the slat and flap

252
00:17:11.606 --> 00:17:15.791
motors, so that what is left goes
to the primary flight controls.

253
00:17:15.841 --> 00:17:19.593
Five: the crew pushes the
engine 1 fire pushbutton.

254
00:17:19.643 --> 00:17:23.384
The fire valve in the left
pylon closes, the engine-driven

255
00:17:23.424 --> 00:17:27.231
pump loses its fluid and shows
LO, and the PTU can still

256
00:17:27.271 --> 00:17:31.533
pressurise Green from Yellow,
transferring power, not fluid.

257
00:17:31.583 --> 00:17:36.519
Six: on the ground, the cargo doors are
operated with the Yellow electric pump.

258
00:17:36.569 --> 00:17:40.829
The PTU is inhibited, the Yellow leak
measurement valve closes and the

259
00:17:40.869 --> 00:17:45.189
flap computer is told not to move the
flaps; these happen together, which

260
00:17:45.229 --> 00:17:50.177
is the trap of statement b in quiz
question 5, but none causes the others.

261
00:17:51.827 --> 00:17:56.798
As in part one, pause after each
statement, decide, then listen.

262
00:17:56.848 --> 00:18:02.655
One: the engine-driven pumps give
3000 psi as soon as the engines turn.

263
00:18:02.705 --> 00:18:06.510
False: they turn with the engine
but only deliver pressure when

264
00:18:06.550 --> 00:18:11.112
the ENG PUMP pushbutton is not at
OFF, which tilts the swashplate.

265
00:18:11.162 --> 00:18:15.076
Two: the PTU can move fluid
from Yellow to Green.

266
00:18:15.126 --> 00:18:19.493
False: it transfers power
through its shaft, never fluid.

267
00:18:19.543 --> 00:18:23.992
Three: the Green reservoir low
level warning is at 3 litres.

268
00:18:24.042 --> 00:18:24.903
True.

269
00:18:24.953 --> 00:18:29.784
Four: the Green relief
valve opens at 3200 psi.

270
00:18:29.834 --> 00:18:35.443
False: it opens at 3500
and closes again at 3200.

271
00:18:35.493 --> 00:18:40.476
Five: the leak measurement valves are
normally closed and opened for tests.

272
00:18:40.526 --> 00:18:43.067
False: they are normally open, feeding the

273
00:18:43.107 --> 00:18:46.530
flight controls, and are
closed for the leak test.

274
00:18:46.580 --> 00:18:53.896
Six: the accumulator is pre-charged with
nitrogen at 1885 psi at 20 degrees.

275
00:18:53.946 --> 00:18:54.923
True.

276
00:18:54.973 --> 00:19:01.093
Seven: the Green pump LO
indication appears below 1740 psi.

277
00:19:01.143 --> 00:19:02.004
True.

278
00:19:02.054 --> 00:19:06.258
Eight: the reservoir air normally
comes from the pneumatic manifold.

279
00:19:06.308 --> 00:19:12.127
False: it normally comes from stage 8 of
engine 1, and the manifold is the backup.

280
00:19:13.777 --> 00:19:17.772
Question 4 of the 2020
quiz, the false statements.

281
00:19:17.822 --> 00:19:20.453
Statement a is true:
the nose-wheel steering

282
00:19:20.493 --> 00:19:23.757
is a Green user, fed
through the priority valve.

283
00:19:23.807 --> 00:19:27.219
Statement b is false: there is
a single filling point, the

284
00:19:27.259 --> 00:19:31.657
Green service panel, with a selector
valve for the three reservoirs.

285
00:19:31.707 --> 00:19:35.692
Statement c is true: the ram air
turbine drives a Blue pump and,

286
00:19:35.732 --> 00:19:40.485
through the constant speed motor
generator, provides electrical power.

287
00:19:40.535 --> 00:19:46.161
Statement d speaks of hydraulic transfer:
the conditions are right, AUTO, 500

288
00:19:46.201 --> 00:19:51.497
psi, inhibited during engine start and
cargo door operation, but what the PTU

289
00:19:51.537 --> 00:19:56.305
transfers is power, through its shaft,
never fluid, since the systems are

290
00:19:56.345 --> 00:20:01.899
independent; read as power transfer, which
is clearly what is meant, d is true.

291
00:20:01.949 --> 00:20:05.299
So the false statement is b,
and if your examiner reads

292
00:20:05.339 --> 00:20:09.058
transfer literally as fluid, d
as well: write one line saying

293
00:20:09.098 --> 00:20:13.700
the PTU transfers power, not fluid,
and you are covered either way.

294
00:20:15.350 --> 00:20:18.127
Question 5 asks for the
correct statements,

295
00:20:18.167 --> 00:20:21.273
and requires the logic
of the PTU manifold.

296
00:20:21.323 --> 00:20:23.660
Statement a is wrong: arming the PTU has

297
00:20:23.700 --> 00:20:26.654
nothing to do with the
leak measurement valve.

298
00:20:26.704 --> 00:20:31.057
Statement b is wrong too, and it is
a trap: during cargo door operation

299
00:20:31.097 --> 00:20:35.573
with the Yellow electric pump, the
PTU is inhibited and the Yellow leak

300
00:20:35.613 --> 00:20:40.027
measurement valve closes at the same
time, but neither causes the other,

301
00:20:40.067 --> 00:20:45.305
and the PTU is never inhibited because
a Green leak measurement valve closes.

302
00:20:45.355 --> 00:20:51.481
Statement c is correct: the Green
pressure switch, which trips at 1450 psi,

303
00:20:51.521 --> 00:20:55.478
informs the brake and steering control
unit, and with Green below that

304
00:20:55.518 --> 00:21:00.322
value the normal brakes are lost and the
alternate brakes on Yellow take over.

305
00:21:00.372 --> 00:21:04.804
Statement d is correct: 800
psi is more than the 500 psi

306
00:21:04.844 --> 00:21:08.143
threshold, so the Yellow system
drives the PTU motor, and

307
00:21:08.183 --> 00:21:11.483
the PTU pump on the Green side
draws fluid from the Green

308
00:21:11.523 --> 00:21:15.269
reservoir and delivers it into
the Green high pressure line.

309
00:21:15.319 --> 00:21:19.063
So the correct statements are
c and d; there is no official

310
00:21:19.103 --> 00:21:23.622
key, so in the exam add one line
of justification to each choice.

311
00:21:25.272 --> 00:21:28.826
To finish, the hydraulic
numbers in one table.

312
00:21:28.876 --> 00:21:33.780
3000 psi in every system, 500 psi
of pressure difference for the

313
00:21:33.820 --> 00:21:38.326
PTU, 50 psi in the reservoirs with
a warning at 22, and the Green

314
00:21:38.366 --> 00:21:43.774
reservoir of 18 litres maximum, 14
normal, with a low level warning at 3.

315
00:21:43.824 --> 00:21:51.302
1740 psi for the pump low pressure
indication, 1450 psi for the manifold

316
00:21:51.342 --> 00:21:58.389
pressure switch, 3500 to open and 3200
to close for the relief valve, 87 and

317
00:21:58.429 --> 00:22:05.757
29 psi for the filters, and 1885 psi of
nitrogen in the one-litre accumulator.

318
00:22:05.807 --> 00:22:08.747
And the RAT, the Blue
emergency source, deployed

319
00:22:08.787 --> 00:22:13.111
automatically on electrical loss
or with its guarded pushbutton.

320
00:22:13.161 --> 00:22:18.037
With the three lectures you have the
whole exam scope: redo the 2020 quiz

321
00:22:18.077 --> 00:22:22.533
without looking, explain the TLT, the
TCT and the crossbleed aloud, and

322
00:22:22.573 --> 00:22:27.780
sketch the bleed circuit and the Green
system from memory; good luck on Thursday.
